# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values (Supabase project
# Settings > API). Never commit .env.local or paste the service role key
# anywhere public — it has full read/write access to the database.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Real per-user login (see AUTH_AND_PERMISSIONS_PLAN.md, Phase 1). Same
# project, so NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL is the same value as SUPABASE_URL
# above — the NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY is a different key, the
# project's "anon" / "publishable" key from that same Settings > API page.
# These are safe to expose to the browser: the anon key only grants what
# Row Level Security allows, which today is nothing on every table, so
# this pair is for identity (who's signed in) only, never for reading or
# writing application data.
#
# Leave both unset to keep the site open with no login required, the same
# as today — until they're set, nothing is gated. Once set, run
# supabase/migrations/0031_profiles.sql (if you haven't already) and
# create your own first account from the Supabase dashboard under
# Authentication > Users > Add user (there's no self-service sign-up).
#
# Also update the "Invite user" and "Reset Password" templates under
# Authentication > Email Templates in the same dashboard — both need their
# link changed from the default {{ .ConfirmationURL }} to:
#   {{ .SiteURL }}/auth/confirm?token_hash={{ .TokenHash }}&type=invite
# (use type=recovery in the "Reset Password" template instead). The
# default links straight to Supabase's own hosted /auth/v1/verify
# endpoint, which consumes the link's one-time token the instant it's
# requested — and most email clients/corporate security gateways prefetch
# every link in an email to scan it, which silently burns through that
# token before the recipient ever clicks anything, so the link shows
# "otp_expired" on the very first real click. Routing through this app's
# own /auth/confirm instead defers that consumption behind a deliberate
# button click (see src/app/auth/confirm/page.tsx), which a prefetch's
# inert page load doesn't trigger. While there, also check Authentication
# > URL Configuration > Site URL itself is set to your real deployed URL
# (e.g. https://specibase.vercel.app), not localhost — that's what
# {{ .SiteURL }} resolves to in the templates above.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
